// Arithmetic in GF(2^255 - 19), the base field Curve25519 (RFC 7748) and
// edwards25519 (RFC 8032) both share. Written from scratch for this
// language, and shared by `x25519` and `y25519` -- er, `ed25519` -- the way
// the one field belongs to both curves that use it.
//
// This module is the SECURITY-CRITICAL core of both: every operation here
// runs on secret data at some point (an X25519 ladder step, an Ed25519
// signing scalar multiplication), so every place this file relies on
// something being constant-time says so, in a comment, at the line that
// relies on it. Read that before changing anything below.
//
// --- representation: 16 limbs, radix 2^16 -----------------------------------
//
// A field element is an `Array<int> of 16, value = sum(limb[i] * 2^(16*i))`.
// 16*16 = 256 bits of capacity for a 255-bit prime -- deliberately uniform
// (unlike the mixed 25.5-bit-per-limb layout some other Curve25519 code
// uses), because a uniform radix needs no per-limb weight correction in the
// multiply, which is one whole source of off-by-one bugs this does not have
// to get right. It also means a 32-byte encoding is exactly two bytes per
// limb, with no leftover bits to special-case.
//
// Every function here returns a FULLY NORMALIZED element: every limb in
// [0, 65535]. Nothing is ever left "lazily reduced" between calls, which
// costs some carry passes but means every function's precondition on its
// arguments is the same as its postcondition on its result -- one invariant,
// checked nowhere because it is always true.
//
// --- constant time: what this file promises, and how ------------------------
//
// The two operations a secret value ever goes through are the four basic
// operations (+ - * squaring) and the carry chain that normalizes them. All
// four of those must take the same time and touch the same memory whatever
// the secret bits are. Concretely:
//
//   - No comparison, `if`, `while` condition or array index in this file
//     ever depends on a field element's value. Every loop bound below is a
//     fixed count (16, 31, 254, ...) known at compile time; every `Array`
//     index is a loop counter, never a value read out of a limb.
//
//   - **`>>` is NOT safe on a value that might be negative, and this is the
//     one non-obvious fact this whole file is built around.** The runtime's
//     `>>` (runtime/rt.h, `rt_ishr`) is
//
//         a < 0 ? ~(~a >> n) : a >> n
//
//     which branches on the SIGN of `a`. That branch is invisible from
//     inside this language -- `>>` looks like one operation -- but the
//     emitted C is a real `a < 0 ? ... : ...`, and at -O0 (which this
//     project's own gates require every emitted program to build clean
//     under) that is an actual conditional branch, not a predication that
//     disappears. If `a` is a field-element limb derived from a secret
//     scalar, the branch taken depends on the secret. This file therefore
//     NEVER calls `>>` on a value that is not first proven, by construction,
//     to be non-negative -- see `carry_pass` and `sub_p_borrow` below, which
//     add a fixed public bias before every `>>` for exactly this reason.
//     Everywhere else, `>>` here is a public, compile-time-constant shift of
//     16 -- the exact same operation, just proven safe first.
//
//   - `+ - *` on `int` trap on overflow (reference §6.1) via a
//     branch on the CPU's overflow flag (runtime/rt.h, `rt_iadd` etc.). That
//     branch's OUTCOME never depends on secret data here, because every
//     bound in this file (see the size comments on `mul_raw`) keeps every
//     intermediate value far below 2^63 -- the overflow check always takes
//     the same "did not overflow" path, for every legal input, so it carries
//     no information about which secret input it was. This is unlike the
//     `>>` case above: there, the two branch outcomes are both reachable by
//     design (a limb really can be negative or not); here, only one ever is.
//
//   - `& | ^ ~` never trap and compile to a plain, unconditional C operator
//     (`rt_iand` etc., `static inline`, no branch of any kind) -- these are
//     the operations `cselect` below is built from, and they are the only
//     genuinely branch-free primitive this language offers.
//
// What this file does NOT independently verify: that gcc/clang, across every
// version and -O level, actually emit branch-free machine code for the
// overflow-checked add/sub/mul (`__builtin_*_overflow`) and for the masked
// `cselect` pattern, rather than something that LOOKS branch-free in C but a
// specific compiler turns back into a branch or a secret-dependent memory
// access. That is a real, known-hard problem for constant-time C in general
// (see the "report" this module ships with) and this file does the one thing
// under its own control -- never write a branch, a comparison or an index
// that a compiler would have to remove -- and no more than that.

// --- construction and encoding ----------------------------------------------

/// The additive identity.
pub Array<int> zero() {
    return [0; 16];
}

/// The multiplicative identity.
pub Array<int> one() {
    Array<int> out = [0; 16];
    out[0] = 1;
    return out;
}

/// The element `number` names, for the handful of small constants the curves need
/// (486662's Montgomery `(A-2)/4`, the `2*d` of the twisted Edwards curve, ...).
/// `number` must fit in 32 bits, which is every constant this module is ever
/// asked to build.
pub Array<int> small(int number) {
    Array<int> out = [0; 16];
    out[0] = number & 0xFFFF;
    out[1] = number >> 16 & 0xFFFF;
    return out;
}

/// The field element 32 little-endian octets encode, taken as a raw 256-bit
/// load -- exactly two octets per limb, with NO masking of any bit.
///
/// Both RFC 7748 (the u-coordinate's top bit) and RFC 8032 (the sign bit
/// riding on the y-coordinate's encoding) reserve the top bit of the last
/// octet for something that is not field data; masking it off is the
/// caller's job, on the raw bytes, before this function ever sees them --
/// this function only knows how to load 256 bits into 16 limbs.
pub Array<int> from_bytes(bytes encoded) {
    Array<int> out = [0; 16];
    for (int limb_index in 0 .. 16) {
        out[limb_index] = encoded[2 * limb_index] | encoded[2 * limb_index + 1] << 8;
    }
    return out;
}

/// `element`, canonically reduced to `[0, p)` and packed as 32 little-endian
/// octets -- the encoding RFC 7748 and RFC 8032 both specify.
///
/// This runs on secret data (an X25519 shared secret, an Ed25519 nonce
/// point) and its constant-time argument is `reduce_canonical`'s.
pub bytes to_bytes(Array<int> element) {
    Array<int> canonical = reduce_canonical(element);
    bytes out = [0; 32];
    for (int limb_index in 0 .. 16) {
        out[2 * limb_index] = canonical[limb_index] & 0xFF;
        out[2 * limb_index + 1] = canonical[limb_index] >> 8 & 0xFF;
    }
    return out;
}

// --- the carry chain: the one place every operation below bottoms out at ----

// 2^256 = 2 * 2^255 = 2*(p + 19) = 2p + 38, so a carry OUT of limb 15
// (weight 2^240, the top limb) by an amount `c` represents `c * 2^256`,
// which is `c * 38 (mod p)`, folded back into limb 0.
const int WRAP = 38;
// A public bias, and its exact quotient by 2^16 -- both compile-time
// constants, never secret. `BIAS` only has to exceed the largest magnitude
// any limb this file ever builds could go negative by; measured, that is
// under 2^17 (an elementwise subtraction of two normalized limbs). `BIAS` is
// four billion, an enormous margin over that, chosen as a round power of two
// so `BIAS_HI` is exact. Adding it to an already-huge NON-negative value (as
// `mul_raw`'s output can be, up to roughly 2^42) changes nothing but the sign
// safety margin -- it is still added unconditionally, every time, which is
// the point: this file does not ask which case it is in.
const int BIAS = 1 << 32;
const int BIAS_HI = BIAS >> 16;

// One left-to-right ripple carry over 16 limbs, folding a carry out of the
// top back in at 38x. Every input limb may be any `int` at all -- negative,
// or far above 2^16 -- and every output limb is in [0, 65535].
//
// CONSTANT TIME: `biased` is `fe[i] + BIAS`, which is positive for every
// value this file ever calls it with (see `BIAS` above), so the `>>` below
// always takes the branch for a non-negative operand, regardless of whether
// `fe[i]` itself was negative, zero, or large -- the two cases this function
// exists to handle are indistinguishable to the CPU that runs it.
Array<int> carry_pass(Array<int> element) {
    Array<int> out = clone(element);
    for (int i in 0 .. 16) {
        int biased = out[i] + BIAS;
        int quotient = biased >> 16;
        out[i] = biased - (quotient << 16);
        int carry = quotient - BIAS_HI;
        if (i < 15) {
            out[i + 1] = out[i + 1] + carry;
        } else {
            out[0] = out[0] + WRAP * carry;
        }
    }
    return out;
}

// Three ripple-carry passes, always enough to bring every limb into
// [0, 65535] from anything this module produces (verified by differential
// testing against Python's arbitrary-precision arithmetic -- see the test
// harness) -- the value it represents may still be a small multiple of `p`,
// not yet the canonical representative; only `reduce_canonical` produces
// that.
Array<int> normalize(Array<int> element) {
    Array<int> first_pass = carry_pass(element);
    Array<int> second_pass = carry_pass(first_pass);
    return carry_pass(second_pass);
}

// --- the four operations -----------------------------------------------------

pub Array<int> add(Array<int> left, Array<int> right) {
    Array<int> out = [0; 16];
    for (int i in 0 .. 16) {
        out[i] = left[i] + right[i];
    }
    return normalize(out);
}

pub Array<int> sub(Array<int> left, Array<int> right) {
    Array<int> out = [0; 16];
    for (int i in 0 .. 16) {
        out[i] = left[i] - right[i];
    }
    return normalize(out);
}

pub Array<int> neg(Array<int> element) {
    return sub(zero(), element);
}

// The schoolbook convolution of two normalized elements (every input limb in
// [0, 65535]), folded once at 38x, NOT yet carry-propagated.
//
// Size bound, checked by the differential tests and relied on by the
// "overflow trap never fires on legal input" argument in the header: a term
// `a[i]*b[j]` is at most (2^16-1)^2 < 2^32; up to 16 such terms land in one
// `t[k]` before folding, so `t[k] < 2^36` going into the fold; the fold adds
// `38 * t[k]` (< 2^42) into a `t[k-16]` that already held < 2^36, so every
// entry handed to `normalize` is a non-negative value under 2^43 -- fifteen
// bits of headroom below `int`'s trap threshold before it is even carried.
Array<int> mul_raw(Array<int> left, Array<int> right) {
    Array<int> product = [0; 31];
    for (int i in 0 .. 16) {
        int left_limb = left[i];
        for (int j in 0 .. 16) {
            product[i + j] = product[i + j] + left_limb * right[j];
        }
    }
    int fold_index = 30;
    while (fold_index > 15) {
        product[fold_index - 16] = product[fold_index - 16] + WRAP * product[fold_index];
        fold_index = fold_index - 1;
    }
    return product.slice(0, 16);
}

pub Array<int> mul(Array<int> left, Array<int> right) {
    return normalize(mul_raw(left, right));
}

pub Array<int> square(Array<int> element) {
    return mul(element, element);
}

/// `2*a*a` -- the extended-coordinates addition law wants this exact shape
/// as its own step often enough (`ed25519.m31`) to give it a name; it is
/// `add(sq(a), sq(a))` and nothing more.
pub Array<int> sq2(Array<int> element) {
    Array<int> squared = square(element);
    return add(squared, squared);
}

// --- constant-time selection -------------------------------------------------

/// `when_one` if `cond` is 1, `when_zero` if `cond` is 0 -- with no branch, for any two
/// normalized elements. `cond` must be exactly 0 or 1; anything else is a
/// bug in the caller (not checked, the way an out-of-range index is not
/// re-checked by every caller of `Array` indexing).
///
/// CONSTANT TIME: `mask` is `0 - cond`, which is 0 or -1 (all 64 bits set,
/// two's complement) -- `-` never traps here because 0-0 and 0-1 both fit
/// trivially, so the overflow check inside `-` always takes its "no
/// overflow" path regardless of which of the two secret values `cond` is.
/// `&`, `|` and `^` (inside `~`, `wrapping_` needed nowhere) are the
/// runtime's plain, unconditional bitwise operators (see the header): the
/// whole selection is arithmetic on 64-bit patterns, never a read of `cond`
/// by anything that could branch on it.
pub Array<int> cselect(int cond, Array<int> when_one, Array<int> when_zero) {
    int mask = 0 - cond;
    int notmask = mask ^ -1;
    Array<int> out = [0; 16];
    for (int i in 0 .. 16) {
        out[i] = when_one[i] & mask | when_zero[i] & notmask;
    }
    return out;
}

/// Swap `first` and `second` in place when `cond` is 1, leave them alone when it is
/// 0 -- the Montgomery ladder's `cswap`. `Array` is a reference type
/// (reference §3.9), so `first` and `second` here are the caller's own arrays: this
/// mutates them directly, element by element, rather than building and
/// returning new ones. Same masking argument as `cselect`.
pub void cswap(int cond, Array<int> first, Array<int> second) {
    int mask = 0 - cond;
    int notmask = mask ^ -1;
    for (int i in 0 .. 16) {
        int first_limb = first[i];
        int second_limb = second[i];
        first[i] = first_limb & notmask | second_limb & mask;
        second[i] = first_limb & mask | second_limb & notmask;
    }
}

// --- canonical reduction: exactly two conditional subtractions --------------

const Array<int> P_LIMBS = [
    0xFFED, 0xFFFF, 0xFFFF, 0xFFFF, 0xFFFF, 0xFFFF, 0xFFFF, 0xFFFF,
    0xFFFF, 0xFFFF, 0xFFFF, 0xFFFF, 0xFFFF, 0xFFFF, 0xFFFF, 0x7FFF
];

// A public bias for the borrow chain, sized the same way as `BIAS` above:
// comfortably larger than the at-most-~2^17 magnitude a borrowing
// subtraction of two normalized, canonical-range limbs can ever produce.
const int SBIAS = 1 << 20;
const int SBIAS_HI = SBIAS >> 16;

// A pair with no other reason to be public: `v - p`'s 16-limb difference and
// the borrow out of the top limb.
type Borrowed {
    Array<int> diff;
    int borrow;
}

/// `v - p`, elementwise, with an explicit borrow chain -- NOT folded at 38x,
/// because the question this answers is "was `element` below `p`", which the fold
/// would hide. Returns the 16-limb difference and the final borrow: 1 means
/// `v < p` (the difference is garbage, `v - p + 2^256`, and must be
/// discarded); 0 means `v >= p` (the difference is the true `v - p`).
///
/// CONSTANT TIME: same technique as `carry_pass`. `difference` ranges over roughly
/// [-65536, 65535] (two canonical-range limbs and a 0/1 borrow-in), so
/// `dd + SBIAS` is always positive, and the `>>` below never branches on the
/// secret sign of `difference` itself.
Borrowed sub_p_borrow(Array<int> element) {
    Array<int> out = [0; 16];
    int borrow = 0;
    for (int i in 0 .. 16) {
        int difference = element[i] - P_LIMBS[i] - borrow;
        int biased = difference + SBIAS;
        int quotient = biased >> 16;
        out[i] = biased - (quotient << 16);
        borrow = SBIAS_HI - quotient;
    }
    return Borrowed(out, borrow);
}

// `element` is always the output of `normalize`, so every limb is in [0, 65535]
// and the value it represents is under 2^256. Since 2*p = 2^256 - 38, that
// value minus 2*p is at most (2^256-1) - (2^256-38) = 37 -- strictly less
// than `p` -- so subtracting `p` twice, each time only if the subtraction
// does not borrow, always lands in [0, p). No more, and no fewer, than two
// subtractions are ever needed, so this is two fixed steps, not a loop
// whose length could depend on the secret value.
Array<int> reduce_canonical(Array<int> element) {
    Array<int> reduced = element;
    for (int step in 0 .. 2) {
        Borrowed subtraction = sub_p_borrow(reduced);
        reduced = cselect(1 - subtraction.borrow, subtraction.diff, reduced);
    }
    return reduced;
}

// --- inversion and the square-root helper: fixed exponents, by squaring ----
//
// Both exponents below are FIXED public constants (they name the field, not
// any input), so unlike everything above, there would be nothing wrong with
// a variable-length addition chain here -- the reason this uses a plain
// square-and-multiply over every bit instead of a shorter, hand-picked chain
// (as e.g. ref10's fe_invert.c does) is simply that a 255-bit loop with two
// documented skipped positions is far easier to independently verify against
// the exponent's own binary expansion than an addition chain is to check by
// eye, and inversion is not called often enough for the difference to matter.
//
// `p - 2 = 2^255 - 21`. For any n-bit X, `(2^n - 1) - X == (2^n - 1) XOR X`
// (subtracting X from all-ones borrows nowhere), so with X = 20 = 0b10100:
// the 255-bit binary expansion of `p - 2` is 255 ones with bits 2 and 4
// cleared. Square-and-multiply from the top bit (254, folded into the
// initial value) down to bit 0 is 254 squarings, multiplying by the base at
// every bit except 2 and 4.

/// `a^(p-2) = a^-1` for any nonzero `element` (undefined, like any inverse, at
/// zero -- callers that can see zero must decide what that means to them).
pub Array<int> invert(Array<int> element) {
    Array<int> power = element;
    int bit = 253;
    while (bit >= 0) {
        power = square(power);
        if (bit != 2 && bit != 4) {
            power = mul(power, element);
        }
        bit = bit - 1;
    }
    return power;
}

// `(p - 5) / 8 = 2^252 - 3`. With X = 2 = 0b10: the 252-bit expansion of
// `(p-5)/8` is 252 ones with bit 1 cleared. 251 squarings below the initial
// (implicit) top bit, multiplying at every bit except 1.

/// `a^((p-5)/8)` -- the candidate exponent RFC 8032 §5.1.3 uses to compute a
/// square root mod p (since p = 5 mod 8): the caller still has to check the
/// candidate and correct it by `sqrt(-1)` if needed, exactly as the RFC
/// describes. Not a square root on its own.
pub Array<int> pow_p_minus_5_over_8(Array<int> element) {
    Array<int> power = element;
    int bit = 250;
    while (bit >= 0) {
        power = square(power);
        if (bit != 1) {
            power = mul(power, element);
        }
        bit = bit - 1;
    }
    return power;
}

// --- equality and parity -----------------------------------------------------

/// Whether `left` and `right` are the same field element. Compares canonical
/// encodings, so two representations of the same value always agree; this
/// is used on public values only (a decoded point's coordinates against a
/// recomputed candidate) and makes no constant-time claim -- `==` on `bytes`
/// is ordinary byte comparison.
pub bool eq(Array<int> left, Array<int> right) {
    return to_bytes(left) == to_bytes(right);
}

pub bool is_zero(Array<int> element) {
    return eq(element, zero());
}

/// The low bit of `element`'s canonical representative -- the "sign" RFC 8032
/// packs into a point encoding's spare bit. Not a constant-time operation
/// (it reads a public coordinate, never a secret scalar).
pub int parity(Array<int> element) {
    bytes encoded = to_bytes(element);
    return encoded[0] & 1;
}
